### The Implementation

Here is the complete JavaScript implementation using the **Lomuto partition scheme** (where we use the last element as the pivot).

```javascript
/**
 * Main QuickSort function
 * @param {Array} arr - The array to be sorted
 * @param {number} low - Starting index
 * @param {number} high - Ending index
 */
function quickSort(arr, low = 0, high = arr.length - 1) {
    // Base case: If the range is valid (at least two elements)
    if (low < high) {
        // 1. Partition the array and get the pivot's final index
        const pivotIndex = partition(arr, low, high);

        // 2. Recursively sort elements before and after the pivot
        // Sort the left side
        quickSort(arr, low, pivotIndex - 1);
        // Sort the right side
        quickSort(arr, pivotIndex + 1, high);
    }
    return arr;
}

/**
 * Helper function to partition the array
 */
function partition(arr, low, high) {
    // We'll choose the last element as our pivot point
    const pivot = arr[high];

    // i tracks the "boundary" of elements known to be smaller than the pivot
    let i = low - 1;

    for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
        // If the current element is smaller than or equal to the pivot
        if (arr[j] <= pivot) {
            i++; // Move the boundary forward
            // Swap the current element into the "smaller than pivot" section
            [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
        }
    }

    // Finally, swap the pivot itself into its correct sorted position
    [arr[i + 1], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[i + 1]];

    // Return the position where the pivot ended up
    return i + 1;
}

// --- Example Usage ---
const myData = [24, 9, 2, 15, 7, 1, 10];
console.log("Original:", myData);

const sortedData = quickSort([...myData]); 
console.log("Sorted:", sortedData);
```

### Complexity Analysis
Understanding the performance of an algorithm is crucial for choosing the right tool for the job.

<div content-section="important_takeaways">
<ul>
    <li><strong>Average Time Complexity:</strong> <tex>$$O(n \log n)$$</tex> — This is very fast and makes QuickSort one of the most used sorting algorithms in practice.</li>
    <li><strong>Worst-case Time Complexity:</strong> <tex>$$O(n^2)$$</tex> — This happens if the pivot is always the smallest or largest element (e.g., sorting an already sorted array).</li>
    <li><strong>Space Complexity:</strong> <tex>$$O(\log n)$$</tex> — It is an in-place algorithm, meaning it doesn't require much extra memory.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div content-section="common_mistakes">
<strong>Recursive Depth:</strong> Forgetting the base case <code>(low < high)</code> will cause a "Maximum call stack size exceeded" error because the function will keep calling itself forever.
</div>

